WEBVTT

00:00.570 --> 00:02.180
- [Instructor] The focus
of this video is going

00:02.180 --> 00:04.150
to be on sequences,

00:04.150 --> 00:05.980
which you have hopefully already seen.

00:05.980 --> 00:07.210
If you don't know what a sequence is,

00:07.210 --> 00:09.870
I encourage you to review
those videos on Khan Academy.

00:09.870 --> 00:11.630
But we're going to focus on

00:11.630 --> 00:13.980
how we can generate the same sequence

00:13.980 --> 00:18.220
with different functions
that have different domains.

00:18.220 --> 00:20.850
So let's just start with
an example sequence.

00:20.850 --> 00:22.780
Let's say we have a sequence.

00:22.780 --> 00:23.613
It's a six.

00:23.613 --> 00:24.850
You could call that the first term.

00:24.850 --> 00:27.100
Some people would call
that the zeroth term, six.

00:27.100 --> 00:28.430
And then if that's the first term,

00:28.430 --> 00:31.720
the second term is now a 12,

00:31.720 --> 00:34.530
then a 24,

00:34.530 --> 00:35.590
then a 48,

00:36.890 --> 00:38.420
and so on and so forth.

00:38.420 --> 00:41.530
And as we'll see, there's
multiple function definitions

00:41.530 --> 00:43.770
that could create the sequence.

00:43.770 --> 00:48.240
One way to think about it
is this is six times one.

00:48.240 --> 00:50.670
This is six times two.

00:50.670 --> 00:53.060
This is six times four.

00:53.060 --> 00:55.680
This is six times eight.

00:55.680 --> 00:57.540
So it looks like each term

00:57.540 --> 01:02.280
is six times a power of two.

01:02.280 --> 01:03.130
Let me make that clear.

01:03.130 --> 01:06.370
This one right over here is
six times two to the zero.

01:06.370 --> 01:07.910
That's six times one.

01:07.910 --> 01:11.910
This one over here is six
times two to the first.

01:11.910 --> 01:16.020
This one over here is
six times two squared,

01:16.020 --> 01:17.310
six times four.

01:17.310 --> 01:20.840
This right over here is six times two

01:20.840 --> 01:22.350
to the third.

01:22.350 --> 01:25.280
And so one way to view this is

01:25.280 --> 01:28.160
if you view this as the zeroth term.

01:28.160 --> 01:30.570
We could define a function,

01:30.570 --> 01:32.730
call it a of n,

01:32.730 --> 01:34.960
where n is referring to our index

01:34.960 --> 01:36.460
or which term in the sequence.

01:36.460 --> 01:41.060
And it's equal to six times two,

01:41.060 --> 01:43.640
six times two to the n,

01:43.640 --> 01:45.610
where n starts at zero,

01:45.610 --> 01:47.590
and then it keeps incrementing by one.

01:47.590 --> 01:51.690
So it's really all integers
greater than or equal to zero.

01:51.690 --> 01:54.493
And it's very important
to specify that domain,

01:58.978 --> 02:00.061
where n is an

02:03.837 --> 02:04.754
integer and

02:07.221 --> 02:10.720
n is greater than or equal to zero.

02:10.720 --> 02:13.300
You could see what happens
if n is not an integer.

02:13.300 --> 02:15.930
If you tried to put a 1.5 year

02:15.930 --> 02:17.380
or something like that,
then you're not going

02:17.380 --> 02:19.260
to get one of the terms in the sequence.

02:19.260 --> 02:23.520
And if we don't start at
zero, if we started at one,

02:23.520 --> 02:25.840
then this would be the
first term in the sequences,

02:25.840 --> 02:26.800
which is not what we want.

02:26.800 --> 02:28.190
We want to generate the sequence

02:28.190 --> 02:29.500
that I originally wrote down.

02:29.500 --> 02:32.210
And obviously, if you started
at n equals negative one,

02:32.210 --> 02:35.170
then you're gonna get a different
value for your first term.

02:35.170 --> 02:36.490
So this is one way

02:37.719 --> 02:40.230
to essentially define or create a function

02:40.230 --> 02:42.480
which generates this sequence.

02:42.480 --> 02:46.370
But as we'll see, there
are other ways to do it.

02:46.370 --> 02:48.720
For example, let me do another one.

02:48.720 --> 02:50.470
I'll do it in another color.

02:50.470 --> 02:53.030
Let's say I have b of n.

02:53.030 --> 02:55.313
And let's say I want to,

02:58.263 --> 03:01.160
instead of saying, okay, I'm
gonna start at n equals zero,

03:01.160 --> 03:03.680
and you could kind of view
this as the zeroth term,

03:03.680 --> 03:06.300
I want to start at n equals one.

03:06.300 --> 03:07.380
So what you could do is,

03:07.380 --> 03:11.700
is when you input a one, this
essentially becomes a zero.

03:11.700 --> 03:12.533
How do I do that?

03:12.533 --> 03:13.710
Well, I just subtract one from it.

03:13.710 --> 03:16.520
So I could say six times two

03:16.520 --> 03:19.890
to the n minus one power,

03:19.890 --> 03:24.010
where n is

03:24.010 --> 03:25.150
an integer

03:27.295 --> 03:30.190
and n is greater than

03:30.190 --> 03:31.870
or equal to one.

03:31.870 --> 03:34.720
Notice, now when we put
n equals one in here,

03:34.720 --> 03:37.040
we could maybe call this the first term.

03:37.040 --> 03:38.070
We want to generate a six.

03:38.070 --> 03:38.903
So what happens?

03:38.903 --> 03:41.160
One minus one, we get that zeroth power

03:41.160 --> 03:42.680
that we want right over there.

03:42.680 --> 03:45.880
And so six times two to
the zero is indeed six.

03:45.880 --> 03:47.210
Then when n is equal to two,

03:47.210 --> 03:49.660
it's six times two to the two minus one,

03:49.660 --> 03:51.400
which is just two to the first power.

03:51.400 --> 03:53.740
So it just becomes six times
two, which is equal to 12.

03:53.740 --> 03:56.230
So notice, these are
different function definitions

03:56.230 --> 03:57.800
with different domains,

03:57.800 --> 04:01.630
but they're generating
the exact same sequence.

04:01.630 --> 04:04.610
We could also do it recursively.

04:04.610 --> 04:06.130
And we've seen this in other videos.

04:06.130 --> 04:08.490
We can define a function recursively.

04:08.490 --> 04:09.590
We could say, all right, look,

04:09.590 --> 04:12.218
it looks like each of these terms

04:12.218 --> 04:15.860
in our sequence is
twice the previous term.

04:15.860 --> 04:16.700
So we could,

04:16.700 --> 04:19.290
if we want a recursive
definition for the sequence,

04:19.290 --> 04:21.240
we can define the first term,

04:21.240 --> 04:23.310
or, in this case, we
could say the zeroth term

04:23.310 --> 04:25.630
if we want to start at n equals zero.

04:25.630 --> 04:28.460
T of zero is equal to six.

04:28.460 --> 04:32.370
And then we could say t of n

04:33.280 --> 04:37.360
is equal to two times

04:37.360 --> 04:39.090
t of n minus one,

04:39.090 --> 04:41.340
t of n minus one.

04:41.340 --> 04:46.050
And then this is going to be for,

04:46.050 --> 04:49.290
or maybe I'll write it this way, where

04:50.170 --> 04:53.140
n is an integer

04:54.910 --> 04:58.020
and n is greater than

04:58.020 --> 04:59.670
or equal to zero.

04:59.670 --> 05:01.190
This would also generate the sequence.

05:01.190 --> 05:04.550
When you put n equals zero
here, you'll get that term.

05:04.550 --> 05:06.230
When you get n equals one,

05:06.230 --> 05:09.290
t of one is going to be two

05:09.290 --> 05:11.980
times t of one minus one, t of zero.

05:11.980 --> 05:13.890
In that case, it'd be t of,

05:13.890 --> 05:17.000
or, sorry, it would be two
times t of zero is six.

05:17.000 --> 05:19.380
So two times six, it would get you 12.

05:19.380 --> 05:22.090
Now if you wanted it so
that it generates the six

05:22.090 --> 05:24.523
when n equals one, you
could do it this way.

05:26.220 --> 05:27.670
You could write it,

05:27.670 --> 05:29.750
actually, maybe I should
have kept all of that,

05:29.750 --> 05:32.000
or I'm gonna have to rewrite all of that.

05:32.000 --> 05:35.020
But you could write it this way.

05:35.020 --> 05:38.030
Instead of saying t of
zero is equal to six,

05:38.030 --> 05:40.660
we could write t of one is equal to six.

05:40.660 --> 05:43.000
But now we'd have to
write a different domain,

05:43.000 --> 05:44.928
where n still has to be an integer.

05:44.928 --> 05:49.113
N is an integer.

05:50.100 --> 05:51.840
And now instead of saying n

05:51.840 --> 05:52.950
is greater than or equal to zero,

05:52.950 --> 05:56.510
now n is greater than or equal to one.

05:56.510 --> 05:58.940
So hopefully this video
hits the point home

05:58.940 --> 06:00.650
that there's multiple ways,

06:00.650 --> 06:02.090
either with a traditional,

06:02.090 --> 06:03.530
I guess you would say explicit function

06:03.530 --> 06:06.250
or a recursive function like this.

06:06.250 --> 06:08.560
And even in either of those cases,

06:08.560 --> 06:09.990
you could have different domains

06:09.990 --> 06:11.530
and different function definitions

06:11.530 --> 06:13.550
that generate the same sequence,

06:13.550 --> 06:15.723
but you really have to
think about the domain.
